the nhs is under considerable pressure  we've had a very tough summer uh as we  have continued to treat people with  covid uh being admitted to hospitals a  society has opened up again and we've  begun to see a return to normal near  normal levels of presentations of urgent  and emergent people who need urgent  emergency care and of course as we  started to make inroads uh into  treatments for patients whose care has  been disrupted and delayed during the  pandemic so so it undoubtedly feels  exceptionally busy  in the nhs and our nhs organizations are  telling us that all the time now in  september we saw a reduction in the  number of coveted patients in hospital  down to below 5000 in england as  infection rates went down as jenny  showed you earlier uh in the last few  weeks those infection rates have gone  back up again and we have started to see  again as secretary of state has said an  increase in the number of emissions and  an increase in the number of patients in  hospital so now in england in our main  hospitals we're just over six thousand  as i stand here today six thousand  patients with covid which is back to  where we were in the middle of september  uh and i anticipate that over the next  week or two that number will increase  because infection rates are increasing  and as i've said many times there is a  lag between infection rates and hospital  admissions  now  it's not just covet of course  we  have one eye to the flu season  and we don't know what's going to happen  with flu this year but there is a risk  that we will get more flu back and it  will be worse than previous years  because we missed out a season last year  and there are other viruses uh around us  yet as well around as well and of course  we are continuing to do all that work  around uh the recovery of our elective  and routine services so it is very very  busy uh indeed  we we  work very closely with the department of  health social care around the judgment  of how the nhs is doing and we will  continue to do that as we go into winter  but i think the really critical thing is  that the public can help us here the  public can absolutely help the nhs here  and as the secretary of state has said  there are things that the public can do  that will take the pressure off the nhs  so the first is to remember that for  face masks there is guidance in place if  you are in a crowded environment a  higher risk environment on the tube you  know a building cramp building with with  low ventilation then wear that face mask  it makes a difference it really does and  the second thing of course is the  vaccine program because the vaccine  program is our best protection against  covid and therefore  if you've been invited for a booster and  you haven't had one or if you are  getting your invitation in the next few  weeks then get that booster as quickly  as possible because we know that the  immunity that we get from vaccines will  drop off over time and we don't want  that to occur going into winter so this  is not something  that we  that we just need to leave we can get  the public to help us on this and and  during the last 18 months the pandemic i  think the public has been fantastic in  responding to assist us in the nhs with  pressures and and the public can do it  again by remembering covet is still here  we need to be cautious where appropriate  and we need to get those jabs  when we're offered them
 

